{"compatibility":"minor","safety_note_md":"# SafeStan Safety Note

Compatibility: minor.

Protected data: `successes`.

Generated files: `model_safe.stan`, `data_safe.json`.

Changes: moved the sufficient-statistic observation from `transformed data` to top-level data, because SafeStan only permits top-level protected data on the left side of a sampling statement. In `data_safe.json`, `trials[k]` is computed as the number of trials with `z[n] == k`, and `successes[k]` is computed as the sum of `y[n]` over those trials; for the supplied data this gives `trials = [0, 1, 0]` and `successes = [0, 0, 0]`. The per-arm sufficient-statistic arrays are declared with length `K`. Added `theta ~ uniform(0, 1)`, which is density-neutral for the existing `[0, 1]` constraint and satisfies SafeStan's unique-prior requirement.
","model_safe_stan":"/**
 *
 * Same model as bernoulli-bandits.stan with sufficient stats rather
 * than Bernoulli.
 */
data {
  int<lower=1> K; // num arms
  int<lower=0> N; // num trials
  array[N] int<lower=1, upper=K> z; // arm on trial n
  array[N] int<lower=0, upper=1> y; // reward on trial n
  array[K] int<lower=0> successes; // successes per arm
  array[K] int<lower=0> trials; // trials per arm
}
parameters {
  vector<lower=0, upper=1>[K] theta; // arm return prob
}
model {
  theta ~ uniform(0, 1);
  successes ~ binomial(trials, theta);
}
generated quantities {
  simplex[K] is_best; // uniform over max
  {
    real best_prob = max(theta);
    for (k in 1 : K) {
      is_best[k] = theta[k] >= best_prob;
    }
    is_best /= sum(is_best); // uniform for ties
  }
}
","data_safe_json":"{"K":3,"N":1,"y":[0],"z":[2],"successes":[0,0,0],"trials":[0,1,0]}","protected_variables":["successes"]}
